  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Wave Alternative",
    "body": "Begin by assessing user seats and collaboration requirements. Wave Starter includes zero extra user roles, while Wave Pro allows owners to invite contributors with admin, editor or viewer permissions at $19 per month. In contrast, QuickBooks Online prices access by user tiers from 1 user on Simple Start up to 25 users on Advanced, while Zoho Books provides 3 users on Standard for $20 per month and 5 users on Professional for $50 per month. FreshBooks charges a flat fee of $11 per month for every additional team member.\n\nNext, evaluate transaction volume and payment processing fees. Wave charges 2.9% + $0.60 per credit card transaction on Starter. On a $1,000 customer invoice, that transaction fee equals $29.60. On Wave Pro, the fixed $0.60 fee is removed for the first 10 card transactions each month, which reduces the fee to $29.00 per $1,000 transaction. FreshBooks charges 2.9% + $0.30 for credit card payments and 1% for Automated Clearing House (ACH) bank transfers. Collecting ten $1,000 card payments a month on FreshBooks costs $293.00 in fees, while the same ten payments on Wave Pro cost $290.00.\n\nFinally, inspect whether full accounting records or simple client invoicing is the primary goal. Wave produces full balance sheets, profit and loss statements and sales tax reports. Xero and Zoho Books also provide complete double-entry accounting ledgers with formal bank feeds and reconciliation rules. Tools like Bonsai prioritize customer relationship management (CRM), contract signing, project scheduling and client proposals alongside invoicing, but do not provide a double-entry general ledger.\n\nReview these operational criteria before selecting a plan:\n\n- How many team members require simultaneous access, and do they need restricted viewer roles?\n- Does your workflow require tracking inventory levels, purchase orders or project profitability?\n- Will clients pay by credit card, Amex or bank transfer, and what is your average invoice dollar amount?\n- Do you require optical character recognition (OCR) receipt scanning included in your base plan, or are you willing to pay an add-on fee?\n- Is full double-entry accounting necessary, or do basic billing and expense tracking meet your filing needs?"
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "QuickBooks Online Plans and Multi-User Accounting",
      "body": "QuickBooks Online is Intuit's cloud accounting application designed for growing businesses. Simple Start lists at $38 per month for 1 user and 2 accountant seats, covering income and expense tracking, basic reports, one connected bank and two invoices per month in the plan description. Essentials lists at $85 per month for 3 users, adding bulk invoice creation in beta, enhanced reporting and quarterly tax payment estimates. Plus lists at $140 per month for 5 users, introducing inventory tracking, project profitability and automatic bank reconciliation. Advanced costs $340 per month for up to 25 users, adding custom key performance indicators (KPIs), automated workflows and granular user permissions.\n\nQuickBooks Online fits businesses that have outgrown simple bookkeeping and need advanced workflows like inventory management, purchase orders and multi-user access controls. Wave does not offer inventory tracking or purchase orders on any tier.\n\nThe cost difference between the two products is substantial. Wave Pro billed monthly costs $19 per month, which equals $228 per year. QuickBooks Online Simple Start costs $38 per month, totaling $456 per year. QuickBooks Online Plus costs $140 per month, which totals $1,680 per year. Selecting QuickBooks Online Plus instead of Wave Pro represents an annual subscription difference of $1,452.\n\nQuickBooks Online falls short of Wave on initial affordability. Wave provides unlimited invoicing and bookkeeping for $0 on its Starter plan, whereas QuickBooks Online requires an ongoing monthly fee of at least $38 following its 3-month introductory discount."
    },
    {
      "heading": "FreshBooks Client Limits and Invoicing Tools",
      "body": "FreshBooks is an invoicing and client billing application tailored for service professionals. Lite lists at $23 per month and allows billing up to 5 active clients, along with expense tracking, estimates and tax-time reporting. Plus lists at $43 per month for up to 50 clients, adding client retainers, proposals, receipt scanning and accountant access. Premium lists at $70 per month and supports unlimited billable clients, customized email templates and project profitability tracking. FreshBooks also offers a custom-priced Select tier for businesses with high payment volume.\n\nFreshBooks fits consultants, freelancers and agencies who bill clients based on tracked project hours or fixed milestones and who require integrated client proposals. Wave includes estimates and invoices but lacks built-in proposal creation, client retainers and project profitability metrics.\n\nEvaluating the costs based on client count reveals clear differences. Wave allows unlimited billable clients on both its $0 Starter plan and its $19 monthly Pro plan. A business billing 25 clients lands on FreshBooks Plus at $43 per month, which totals $516 per year. If that business adds 2 team members at $11 per user per month, the FreshBooks subscription rises to $65 per month, or $780 per year. Wave Pro for that same team costs $19 per month with no extra seat fees, saving $552 annually.\n\nFreshBooks falls short of Wave for businesses with large client rosters and lean budgets. A company invoicing 60 clients on FreshBooks must choose the $70 per month Premium tier ($840 annually), whereas Wave supports unlimited clients on its $0 Starter plan."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Xero Features and Invoice Caps",
      "body": "Xero is a double-entry accounting platform supporting bank reconciliation, bill tracking and reporting. In the United States, Early lists at $27 per month and limits activity to 20 invoices, quotes and 5 bills, while providing bank reconciliation, 30-day cash flow forecasting and online invoice payment options. Growing lists at $59 per month, removing the caps on invoices, quotes and bills, and adding automated bill entry, a 60-day cash flow forecast and free ACH for domestic bill payments. Established lists at $97 per month, adding multi-currency transactions, project time and cost tracking, employee expense claims and a 180-day cash flow forecast.\n\nXero fits businesses that need double-entry accounting with bank reconciliation rules, cash flow forecasting and unlimited team member collaboration without per-seat charges. Wave charges $19 per month on Pro to access user permissions and bank transaction categorization.\n\nThe arithmetic shifts based on monthly transaction volume. A business sending 30 invoices per month exceeds the Early plan cap of 20 invoices and must subscribe to Xero Growing at $59 per month, which totals $708 per year. Wave Pro costs $190 per year when billed annually, creating an annual savings of $518 compared to Xero Growing. On regular monthly billing, Xero Early costs $27 x 12 = $324 per year, whereas Wave Starter costs $0.\n\nXero falls short of Wave on entry-level billing allowances. Wave Starter permits unlimited invoices for $0, while Xero Early restricts users to 20 invoices per month at $27 per month."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Zoho Books Free Plan and Scaled Tiers",
      "body": "Zoho Books is a cloud accounting software suite with multiple operational tiers. Its Free plan costs $0 per month for 1 user and 1 accountant, supporting invoices, quotes, expense logging, receipt auto-scans, mileage tracking and online payments. Standard lists at $20 per month ($15 per month billed annually) for 3 users, adding bank feeds, progress invoicing, 1099-MISC and 1099-NEC e-filing and application programming interface (API) access. Professional costs $50 per month ($40 billed annually) for 5 users, introducing purchase orders, multi-currency accounting, timesheet billing, project profitability and inventory tracking.\n\nZoho Books fits small businesses that require advanced features like inventory tracking, progress invoicing and 1099 filing at accessible price points. Wave does not offer inventory management or 1099 filing on either Starter or Pro.\n\nComparing the annual costs demonstrates the value of Zoho Books for multi-user setups. A three-person company on Zoho Books Standard billed annually pays $15 x 12 = $180 per year. Wave Pro billed annually costs $190 per year. On monthly billing, Zoho Books Standard is $20 per month ($240 per year) compared to $19 per month ($228 per year) for Wave Pro. For 5 users needing inventory tools, Zoho Books Professional billed annually costs $40 x 12 = $480 per year.\n\nZoho Books falls short of Wave on interface simplicity. Setting up chart of accounts rules, custom roles, inventory items and API connections in Zoho Books demands significantly more configuration than creating a basic invoice inside Wave."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Bonsai Per-User Pricing for Contracts and Billing",
      "body": "Bonsai is a business management and invoicing platform built for agencies, consultants and freelancers. Bonsai charges on a per-user, per-month schedule. On monthly billing, Basic costs $15, Essentials costs $25, Premium costs $39 and Elite costs $59 per user. On annual billing, the rates drop to $9, $19, $29 and $49 per user per month. Basic covers time tracking, task management, client management (CRM), unlimited projects and unlimited clients. Invoicing, online payments, legal contracts, proposals, appointment scheduling, client portals and expense tracking unlock at the Essentials tier.\n\nBonsai fits freelancers and service firms that want to manage the entire client lifecycle from proposals and signed contracts to appointment scheduling and final billing in a single tool. Wave offers invoicing and estimates but does not provide contract templates, digital signatures or calendar scheduling.\n\nExamining multi-user agency pricing highlights the difference in billing structures. A two-person team using Bonsai Essentials on monthly billing pays 2 x $25 = $50 per month, totaling $600 per year. On annual billing, the same two users pay 2 x $19 = $38 per month, or $456 per year. Wave Pro costs a flat $19 per month ($228 per year) or $190 billed annually regardless of the number of users added. Choosing Wave Pro over Bonsai Essentials saves that two-person team $266 per year on annual billing.\n\nBonsai falls short of Wave on accounting structure. Bonsai provides income and expense tracking but does not include a complete double-entry general ledger, balance sheets or sales tax liability ledgers."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who This Is Not For",
      "body": "Businesses with specific complex operating models should look outside both Wave and standard invoicing applications:\n\n- Companies with full-time bookkeeping needs should evaluate dedicated human services rather than software alone. Wave Advisors provides dedicated bookkeeping services starting from $149 to $199 per month, which includes monthly financial statements and a dedicated bookkeeper.\n- Large enterprises requiring complex enterprise resource planning, hundreds of user seats or deep custom database integrations should consider platforms covered in our wider business software guides.\n- Sellers who need physical retail point-of-sale hardware and unified in-person counter checkout should review retail-focused merchant processors.\n- Businesses evaluating desktop-based accounting tools should note that all providers reviewed on this page operate as cloud-hosted software subscriptions."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Would Change Our Answer on Wave Alternatives",
      "body": "Several pricing moves or functional updates could alter the comparative value of these platforms:\n\n- Wave modifying its $0 Starter plan by placing caps on invoice volume, active clients or bookkeeping records.\n- Wave expanding the discounted 2.9% + $0 credit card rate on Wave Pro beyond the current cap of 10 transactions per month.\n- QuickBooks Online altering the monthly list prices for Simple Start ($38), Essentials ($85) or Plus ($140).\n- Xero removing or adjusting the 20-invoice and 5-bill monthly limit on its $27 Early plan.\n- Zoho Books changing the user allowances on its Standard ($20 per month for 3 users) or Professional ($50 per month for 5 users) tiers.\n- Bonsai adjusting its per-user pricing model or introducing double-entry accounting ledgers to its Essentials tier."
    }
  ],
  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Wave Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "Wave remains the superior option for microbusinesses, sole proprietors and freelancers who want genuine double-entry bookkeeping and unlimited invoicing without a monthly subscription fee. Wave Starter delivers unlimited estimates, invoices, bills and bookkeeping records for $0. Users who accept card payments occasionally can simply absorb the 2.9% + $0.60 per-transaction fee without committing to fixed monthly overhead.\n\nFor businesses wanting automated bank feeds, Wave Pro at $19 per month billed monthly or $190 per year billed annually is more affordable than QuickBooks Online Simple Start at $38 per month or Xero Early at $27 per month. Wave Pro also includes unlimited receipt scanning using optical character recognition (OCR), auto-categorization of transactions and automated late payment reminders sent at 3, 7 and 14 days past the invoice due date.\n\nWave also integrates optional services when needed. Owners can add Wave Advisors bookkeeping starting at $149 per month for monthly reconciliations, payroll starting from $25 or $40 per month, and live chat or email support operating Monday to Friday from 9 AM to 4:45 PM Eastern. For single-operator businesses with straightforward billing, Wave provides unmatched utility per dollar."
  },
  "verdict": "Wave Starter delivers the strongest zero-cost invoicing and bookkeeping package available, while Wave Pro provides automated bank feeds and receipt capture for $19 per month. Choose QuickBooks Online if your operation requires inventory management, purchase orders and granular user roles. Select Xero if you need multi-user collaboration and detailed bank reconciliation rules. Opt for Zoho Books if you want project profitability and inventory tracking at a lower cost, and pick Bonsai if you want proposals, contracts and scheduling bundled with billing.\n\nTo identify the best Wave alternative for your business, calculate your required user seats, count your monthly client invoices, determine whether you need inventory tracking, and compare the matched subscription tiers using our breakdown above.",
